    en: {
      kicker: 'Article · Festivals',
      title: 'San Sebastián 2026: the jury rewards a farewell and the audience votes for Gaza and for Lorca',
      lead: 'The Golden Shell went to “Tender Loving Care”, which at 83 Mike Leigh takes to be his last film and which he accepted by video call from New York. The audience, for its part, gave its two prizes to “La bola negra” and to “NAZA”, the documentary about the algorithms that pick who gets bombed in Gaza. A list of winners that makes more sense read whole.',
      stats: [
        { value: '9.40', label: 'audience score for “La bola negra”, the highest ever for a Spanish film' },
        { value: '3', label: 'prizes for “Tender Loving Care”: film, screenplay and performance' },
        { value: '17', label: 'films competing in the Official Selection' },
        { value: '83', label: 'years old: Mike Leigh, who says goodbye with this film' },
        { value: '2', label: 'audience awards: an epic about Lorca and a documentary about Gaza' },
      ],
      body: [
        'The 74th Zinemaldia ran from 18 to 26 September with seventeen films competing for the Golden Shell and a jury chaired by the American director Ira Sachs. Festivals are almost always told through their top prize, and this year the top prize has a story behind it; but the full list says more than its first line, especially set beside what the people buying tickets voted for.',
        '## Mike Leigh\'s farewell',
        'The Golden Shell went to “Tender Loving Care”, by Mike Leigh, the story of two social workers whose lives are coming apart through their own relationships and their own families — the kind of film Leigh has been making since the seventies: ordinary people, long rehearsal, nothing underlined. It did not take only that one: also the jury prize for best screenplay —his— and the Silver Shell for best lead performance for **Kate O\'Flynn**, shared with Asta Kamma August for “Growth of the Soil”. Three prizes in one go; the most awarded film of the night.',
        'Leigh, who is 83, could not attend the closing ceremony and thanked the festival by video call from New York. He has explained that he has myositis, a condition that limits his muscular movement and makes moving around a shoot very hard, and that this is therefore “probably” his last film. It is hard not to read the prize in that light. But it is worth not turning it into a tribute: they did not give it to a career, they gave it to a film, and the jury finished the job by giving him the screenplay too, which is the least sentimental prize of all.',
        '## The rest of the list',
        'The Silver Shell for best direction went to **Amanda Kernell** for “Brace Your Heart”, a drama about Sámi reindeer herding, the indigenous people of northern Scandinavia she comes from and had already filmed in “Sami Blood”. The special jury prize went to “Border”, a first feature by the Chinese director Ah Biao, cited for its formal daring and its sexual explicitness: the jury spoke of “a major new talent in world cinema”. Best cinematography went to Oskar Dahlsbakken for “Growth of the Soil”, which left Donostia with two.',
        'And **Javier Cámara** took the Silver Shell for best supporting performance in “5 minutos más”, by Javier Ruiz Caldera: a couple in crisis shut themselves away for a weekend in a country house and the world gets stuck in a loop where the last five minutes repeat without end. Cámara plays the man from the rental agency who hands them the keys, a character about whom it is better to say nothing else. The film does not open until 22 January, after Sitges, Abycine and Gijón. In New Directors, “Body of Glass” won.',
        '## What the audience voted for',
        'Here is the interesting part. The Donostia City Audience Award went to **“La bola negra”**, by Los Javis, with a 9.40: the highest score a Spanish film has ever had in that prize. It is the same film that won the audience award in Toronto five days earlier, the one the Academy had just chosen as Spain\'s Oscar candidate and the one that opened here the next day with 4.67 million euros in its first weekend. That the critics split down the middle at Cannes while the audience gives it a 9.40 is not a contradiction: it is the definition of what that film is.',
        'The audience award for best European film went to **“NAZA”**, by the Israelis Yuval Abraham and Rachel Szor, with a 9.29. Abraham is one of the directors of “No Other Land”, the Palestinian-Israeli documentary that won the Oscar in 2025. “NAZA” —the title comes from *nezek agavi*, “collateral damage” in Hebrew military jargon— investigates how artificial intelligence and automated surveillance have been built into the systems that pick targets in Gaza: not the soldier pressing the button, but the program deciding who to mark and how many civilian deaths count as acceptable in each case. It is produced by “The Guardian”, premiered in Venice on 10 September and took the Special Jury Prize there.',
        'Two audience awards. One for a queer epic that starts from four pages Lorca never finished before he was killed; the other for a documentary about the industrial calculation of who may be killed today. A festival is defined as much by what its jury rewards as by what its full house votes for, and what the full house in Donostia voted for this year was the memory of one massacre and the chronicle of another. If that interests you, the archive has a dossier on Palestinian cinema that tells where that tradition of filming what is being erased comes from.',
        '## Herzog and Watts',
        'The honorary Donostia Awards went to **Werner Herzog** and **Naomi Watts**. Herzog is the man who dragged a boat over a mountain in “Fitzcarraldo” and has spent half a century filming people who obsess their way into disaster; Watts has spent another quarter of a century doing exactly the opposite of what is expected of an actress in her position, from “Mulholland Drive” to her recent small films. Two careers with nothing in common except what a festival rewards when it rewards a body of work: having chosen badly on purpose, many times, and having been right.',
        '## What is left',
        'Of all this, in Spain you can see “La bola negra” right now —in cinemas since 25 September and on Netflix on 2 December— and “5 minutos más” on 22 January. As for the rest, the usual: those with a distributor turn up over the winter and some fall by the wayside until a platform or a season rescues them. It is the least reported part of a festival: the winners are announced on Saturday night and half those films take a year to reach a screen where you can actually watch them. Winners and figures checked on 1 October 2026.',
      ],
    },
